<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Standard deviation of grouped data using an assumed mean</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>[[if test="is(w=10)"]]The table shows the time, \(t\) minutes, that \({@n@}\) patients waited at a clinic in Machakos.[[elif test="is(w=20)"]]The table shows the masses, \(m\) kg, of \({@n@}\) bags of charcoal at a depot in Kitui.[[else]]The table shows the daily earnings, \(e\) Ksh, of \({@n@}\) boda boda riders in Eldoret.[[/if]]</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">[[if test="is(w=10)"]]Time (minutes)[[elif test="is(w=20)"]]Mass (kg)[[else]]Earnings (Ksh)[[/if]]</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@lo[1]@} \le {@vl@} \lt {@lo[1]+w@}\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@lo[2]@} \le {@vl@} \lt {@lo[2]+w@}\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@lo[3]@} \le {@vl@} \lt {@lo[3]+w@}\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@lo[4]@} \le {@vl@} \lt {@lo[4]+w@}\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@lo[5]@} \le {@vl@} \lt {@lo[5]+w@}\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@lo[6]@} \le {@vl@} \lt {@lo[6]+w@}\)</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">Frequency (\(f\))</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[6]@}</td></tr></table><p>Use the assumed mean \(A = {@A@}\), with \(d = x - A\), where \(x\) is the class midpoint.</p><p>(a) Find \(\Sigma fd\).</p><div>\(\Sigma fd =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find \(\Sigma fd^2\).</p><div>\(\Sigma fd^2 =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) Estimate the mean, correct to 1 decimal place.</p><div>\(\bar{x} =\) [[input:ans3]] [[if test="is(w=10)"]]minutes[[elif test="is(w=20)"]]kg[[else]]Ksh[[/if]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div><p>(d) Estimate the standard deviation, correct to 2 decimal places. Do not round until the final answer.</p><div>\(\sigma =\) [[input:ans4]] [[if test="is(w=10)"]]minutes[[elif test="is(w=20)"]]kg[[else]]Ksh[[/if]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Subtracting the same number \(A\) from every value moves the mean down by \(A\) and leaves the spread unchanged. So work with \(d = x - A\), where \(x\) is the class midpoint: add \(A\) back to the mean of \(d\), but not to its standard deviation. Using midpoints makes these estimates.</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Class</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\(f\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Midpoint (\(x\))</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\(d = x - {@A@}\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\(fd\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\(fd^2\)</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@lo[1]@} \le {@vl@} \lt {@lo[1]+w@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@mid[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@dv[1]@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@grpm(fd[1])@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@grpm(fd2[1])@}\)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@lo[2]@} \le {@vl@} \lt {@lo[2]+w@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@mid[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@dv[2]@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@grpm(fd[2])@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@grpm(fd2[2])@}\)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@lo[3]@} \le {@vl@} \lt {@lo[3]+w@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@mid[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@dv[3]@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@grpm(fd[3])@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@grpm(fd2[3])@}\)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@lo[4]@} \le {@vl@} \lt {@lo[4]+w@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@mid[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@dv[4]@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@grpm(fd[4])@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@grpm(fd2[4])@}\)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@lo[5]@} \le {@vl@} \lt {@lo[5]+w@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@mid[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@dv[5]@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@grpm(fd[5])@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@grpm(fd2[5])@}\)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@lo[6]@} \le {@vl@} \lt {@lo[6]+w@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[6]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@mid[6]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@dv[6]@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@grpm(fd[6])@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@grpm(fd2[6])@}\)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">Total</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@n@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;"></td><td style="padding:4px 10px;"></td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@grpm(Sfd)@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@grpm(Sfd2)@}\)</td></tr></table><p>(a) \(\Sigma fd = {@grpm(Sfd)@}\). (b) \(\Sigma fd^2 = {@grpm(Sfd2)@}\).</p><p>(c) \[\bar{x} = A + \frac{\Sigma fd}{\Sigma f} = {@A@} [[if test="is(Sfd>=0)"]]+[[else]]-[[/if]] \frac{ {@grpm(aSfd)@} }{ {@n@} } \approx {@mnd@}\]</p><p>(d) \[\sigma^2 = \frac{\Sigma fd^2}{\Sigma f} - \left(\frac{\Sigma fd}{\Sigma f}\right)^2 = \frac{ {@grpm(Sfd2)@} }{ {@n@} } - \left(\frac{ {@grpm(Sfd)@} }{ {@n@} }\right)^2 \approx {@vrd@}\]so \(\sigma = \sqrt{\sigma^2} \approx {@sdd@}\) [[if test="is(w=10)"]]minutes[[elif test="is(w=20)"]]kg[[else]]Ksh[[/if]], using the unrounded variance. The variance of \(x\) equals the variance of \(d\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
